@ 30.1.2 - Pre-Proto-Bantu to Proto-Manenguba
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Hedinger, Robert (1987), The Manenguba Languages (Bantu A.15, Mbo Cluster) of Cameroon NB: In Hedinger’s notation, an apostrophe indicates a lenis consonant in Pre-Proto-Bantu. *ɟ may have turned into one of {c,(n)z}?
	’p > f / in C position
	’t > l / in C position
	’d/d > ɟ / in C position
	c > s / in C position
	{’ɟ} > ∅ / in C position
	’k > {w,∅} / in C position
	ɡ > {k,w} / in C position
	N:[+lenis] > N:[-lenis] / in C position
	p > b / in C position
	t > d / in C position
	’t > l / in C position
	{’d,d} > {l,∅} / in C position
	c > ɟ / in C position
	k > ɡ / in C position
	’k > ∅ / in C position
	{ ’m, m, ’n, n, {’ɲ,ɲ}, ŋ } > { m, {ᵐb,m}, n, {ⁿd,n}, ɲ, {ᵑɡ,ŋ} } / in C position
	{ {u,o}, {ɛ,e,i} } > { w, j } / C_a in noun root͡s
	{ {u,o}, {ɛ,e,i} } > { w, j } / C_(a) in verb root͡s
	{ {u,o}, {ɛ,e,i} } > { w, j } / C_$V in noun class prefixes
	f > h (perhaps not in all languages?)